Irritable Bowel Syndrome (IBS)
Gastrointestinal & Hepatology
Recurrent abdominal pain, Pain related to defecation, Altered bowel habits, Absence of alarm features

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: GIH-041
Reason for Visit: Acute escalation of agonizing abdominal cramps and massive distension following a highly stressful personal event, causing extreme patient distress and fear of a bowel obstruction.
Location: Private residence.
Present at Visit: Patient only.
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted an acute, severe exacerbation of generalized abdominal cramping and immense visible bloating causing extreme distress. Urgent out-of-hours physical assessment was strictly necessary to definitively exclude an acute mechanical bowel obstruction or inflammatory peritonitis, and to provide potent antispasmodic relief to de-escalate the acute crisis prior to standard clinic availability.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My stomach is cramping violently, it is blown up like a balloon, and I alternate between severe constipation and sudden diarrhoea."
History of Present Illness: Patient reports a 4-year history of recurrent abdominal pain and cramping occurring at least 2 days per week on average over the preceding three months. Reports relief or exacerbation of abdominal pain directly related to defecation. Notes a frequent change in the frequency and physical appearance of bowel movements, characterizing a mixed subtype. Reports a constant sensation of incomplete rectal evacuation. Today, following a period of intense psychological stress, patient developed severe, visible abdominal bloating, subjective distension, and agonizing cramps. Notes the passage of clear mucus per rectum. There is a complete absence of alarm features such as rectal bleeding, nocturnal symptoms awakening the patient, or unintentional weight loss.
Functional Status: Ambulating independently but frequently bending over clutching the abdomen during spasms.
Social/Safety Concerns: Severe acute health anxiety; fearful of a catastrophic diagnosis.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 140/85 | HR: 90 | Temp: 36.7 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 72 Kg
General: Patient appears highly anxious, visibly uncomfortable, and frequently massaging the abdomen.
Physical Exam: Abdomen is visibly distended and globally tympanic to percussion. Diffuse, mild tenderness present on deep palpation, particularly in the left lower quadrant along the descending colon. Absolute absence of guarding, rigidity, or localized rebound peritonism. Bowel sounds are active and normal-pitched. Rectal examination is deferred based on the benign abdominal exam and absence of alarm features. The clinical presentation explicitly aligns with recurrent abdominal pain, pain related to defecation, altered bowel habits, and an absolute absence of alarm features.
Environmental Observations: Normal living environment; patient has a heat pack applied to the abdomen.
Medication Reconciliation: Nil regular medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Irritable Bowel Syndrome (Mixed Subtype) - Status: Exacerbated (Acute severe spastic flare)
Differentials:

Inflammatory Bowel Disease - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Complete absence of nocturnal symptoms, overt rectal bleeding, fever, or unintentional weight loss, and the fact that symptoms are strongly linked to psychological stress.

Mechanical Bowel Obstruction - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Passage of stool and flatus today, active normal-pitched bowel sounds, and a soft, non-rigid abdomen upon palpation.

Coeliac Disease - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of profound lethargy, refractory iron deficiency anaemia, or characteristic dermatitis herpetiformis.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with an acute, severe exacerbation of chronic functional bowel symptoms, directly triggered by profound psychological stress. The severity of the cramping and distension mandated urgent out-of-hours evaluation to definitively exclude a surgical crisis. Clinical examination firmly established severe functional gaseous distension and colonic spasm (tympanitic, soft abdomen with normal bowel sounds) and definitively excluded peritonism, obstruction, or mass. The explicit absence of red flag features allowed the clinician to safely de-escalate the emergency, preventing an unnecessary presentation to the Emergency Department overnight. Targeted antispasmodic therapy and reassurance were safely initiated.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribed oral Hyoscine Butylbromide (Buscopan) 20mg immediately, then QID PRN for acute spasms.
Positive Behavioral Change: Apply a hot water bottle specifically to the lower left side of the abdomen and practice deep, slow diaphragmatic breathing for 10 minutes. This combination of localized heat and deep breathing physically interrupts the hyperactive nerve signals causing the severe muscle spasms in the colon, directly overriding the stress response and relaxing the bowel wall.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 2 weeks to discuss long-term management strategies and dietary review.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

If the abdominal pain becomes sudden, continuous, and unbearable (no longer coming in waves), if the abdomen becomes hard like a board, or if repeated vomiting begins, present immediately to the nearest Emergency Department.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: IBS is a functional disorder; the bowel looks normal but functions abnormally due to hypersensitive nerves.
Clinical Course: The extreme cramping will subside completely within the next few hours with the antispasmodic medication and stress reduction.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

The physical examination today showed no signs of a blocked or burst bowel. However, if a high fever develops, fresh blood is passed in the toilet, or if the pain wakes the patient from a deep sleep, medical care must be sought immediately as these are not symptoms of IBS.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The bowel is structurally healthy, but the nerves controlling the muscle wall are highly sensitive to stress. The intense psychological stress experienced triggered an overwhelming response, causing the smooth muscle in the colon to violently spasm and trap normal digestive gas. The prescribed medication works directly on these nerves to paralyze the spasm, instantly releasing the trapped gas and stopping the agonizing cramps.
Actionable Steps: Eliminate all caffeine and highly processed foods for the next 48 hours. Caffeine is a powerful direct stimulant to the bowel muscle; consuming it while the nerves are already hypersensitive will immediately trigger further violent spasms.

